;
DISKS, VOLUMES AND NAMED DIRECTORIES

A - A tour of the disks
B - Putting together a software collection
C - Giving directories names

:A
A TOUR OF THE DISKS

Drive - Exact image bytes - Purpose
  A: - 256256 - Boot and system disk
  I: - 4177920 - Workspace, help, private utilities and board data
  J: - 4177920 - Swappable software collections
  P: - 536870912 - Large swappable archives

The named directories are more useful than the bare drive letters:

  DU - Name - Contents
  A0: - SYSTEM - Public commands, boot files and WELCOME.TXT
  A15: - - - Trusted board executable and static files
  I0: - WORK - Temporary caller workspace
  I13: - SYSUTLS - Private tools, including AUTH, PIP, PPIP, SET and ZDE
  I14: - HELP - Caller-readable online documentation
  I15: - RBBS - Private board records and message base
  P0: - SIGM - SIG/M libraries on the default P:
  P1: - CPMUG - CPMUG libraries on the default P:
  J0: - VOLUME - Default J: volume directory

WORK is cleared before answering, including startup and call recovery. Never
leave your only copy of a rescued edit or a sysop document there. Uploads go
to a separate host inbox, not a permanent CP/M UPLDS: directory.

:B
PUTTING TOGETHER A SOFTWARE COLLECTION

The default P: contains all 310 SIG/M and 93 CPMUG volumes. Browse a few of
them before deciding what else your board ought to offer. You might specialize
in a particular computer, programming language, or collection of games.

Put small raw z80pack-hd images in disks/library/ and large raw z80pack-hdb
images in disks/library/archives/. Catalog names are one to eight letters,
digits or underscores, followed by .dsk. A filename extension is no guarantee
of disk geometry. Prepare images with the matching format in the Disk Images
reference; simply padding or renaming an unrelated image won't convert it.

    MOUNT /L
    MOUNT J: /L
    MOUNT P: /L
    MOUNT GAMES
    MOUNT P: /DEFAULT
    MOUNT J: /DEFAULT

MOUNT chooses J: or P: by exact image size. If a name occurs in both catalogs,
qualify it with the drive. The old image remains mounted if selection or
buffer flushing fails. A successful mount relogs the selected disk and
refreshes its names. Default images return when the emulator restarts, not
necessarily when a caller logs off.

Keep private media outside the public catalogs (the supplied offline directory
is one such place). A private image mounted in a caller-readable DU is no
longer private simply because of where it came from. AUTH permissions follow
the drive/user slot, not the volume.

Never host-edit an image while cpmsim has it open. Stop the board, keep a
backup, and use the supplied disk tools (based on cpmtools). Host file
permissions and CP/M attributes are distinct from AUTH; an owner-writable
image does not by itself grant callers write access.

The GAMES collection has preparation launchers in J0: for Adventure, Animals
and Maze Chase. Try MOUNT GAMES followed by J0:ADVENT, J0:ANIMALS or
J0:MCHASE. The launchers need readable/writable WORK for data; the approved
game itself runs from a protected DU. Games that insist on writing to the
library disk are better left for download and local play.

:C
GIVING DIRECTORIES NAMES

A caller will remember INFOCOM: more readily than J3:. Create an extensionless
marker file named -INFOCOM in the intended user area. Its contents don't
matter. Use up to seven letters, digits or underscores after the hyphen.

    NDSCAN J:

NDSCAN rebuilds names for that drive alone, scanning users 0-15. MOUNT
normally chains to NDSCAN after a swap. Areas without markers lose their old
names. Duplicate names, multiple different markers in one user area,
collisions with other drives or a full name table prevent the update. The
supplied table holds 42 names. See MOUNT and NDSCAN for additional details.

If a mount succeeds but scanning fails, the new disk is mounted and the old
names are still present. Use numeric DUs until you correct the markers and
rerun NDSCAN, or restore the default volume. Callers need read access to every
user area on a drive to scan it; private sysops can scan private drives.

Use PWD or ZSHOW for inspection. Don't use a bare NAME command to inspect a
name: it can delete an entry. Names do not grant access.
